==============================================================
Kaupangr
==============================================================

A small-shop management app for Linux and Windows. Local-first:
every action is written first to a local SQLite database, so the
app works fully offline and responds instantly. A central server
is optional, not required.

--------------------------------------------------------------
What it does
--------------------------------------------------------------

- POS with barcode scanning and live on-hand warnings
- Stores, suppliers, products
- Sales and returns — append-only, never edited
- Stock tracked from movements (purchase, sale, return, adjustment)
- Reports: by day, by store, by seller, by product, and hourly heatmap
- Users with three roles: administrator, product manager, seller
- Bulk price update, CSV import, CSV export
- Receipt printing
- Optional sync with a central server (Postgres)

--------------------------------------------------------------
Install (from source)
--------------------------------------------------------------

Requirements:

  - Node.js 20+
  - A C++ toolchain for better-sqlite3:
      Linux:    sudo apt install build-essential python3
      Windows:  Visual Studio Build Tools

Then:

  git clone <repo-url> kaupangr
  cd kaupangr
  npm install
  npm start

On first run, the app creates its database at:

  Linux:    ~/.config/Kaupangr/kaupangr.db
  Windows:  %APPDATA%\Kaupangr\kaupangr.db

First launch bootstraps a single administrator:

  Login:     admin
  Password:  admin

Change the password after the first sign-in.

--------------------------------------------------------------
Build a distributable
--------------------------------------------------------------

  npm run dist:linux     produces dist/Kaupangr-<version>.AppImage
  npm run dist:win       produces dist/Kaupangr-Setup-<version>.exe

--------------------------------------------------------------
Server (optional)
--------------------------------------------------------------

The server is a small Express app backed by PostgreSQL. It stores
the synchronized items and serves pull requests to other desktops.
You only need it if you want multiple desktops (or multiple shops)
sharing data.

  cd server
  npm install
  DATABASE_URL='postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/kaupangr_dev' npm start

The server exposes two endpoints:

  POST /sync/push
    Receives a batch of items from a desktop.

  GET /sync/pull?since=<iso>&limit=<n>
    Returns items newer than "since".

The contract is intentionally simple: the server does not interpret
the payloads. It stores what a desktop pushes and hands it back
on pull.

Configure sync from the desktop under Settings → Sync:

  1. Set the Server URL.
  2. Tick "Enable synchronization".
  3. Click Save.

Use "Sync now" to push and pull immediately, or leave it: auto-sync
runs every 5 minutes while a server is configured and a user is
signed in.

--------------------------------------------------------------
Roles
--------------------------------------------------------------

Three roles are enforced in the services, not by hiding buttons in
the UI. A seller calling a restricted action from DevTools still
gets ForbiddenError.

  administrator
    Everything, plus user management and settings.

  product_manager
    Everything except user management and settings.

  seller
    POS, sales, returns, reports, product/inventory/supplier/store
    viewing only. No create/edit/deactivate.

Users sign in with a login and password. Only one active session
per user at a time: a new sign-in ends the previous one.

--------------------------------------------------------------
Data model
--------------------------------------------------------------

The interesting guarantees:

  - Sales and returns are append-only. UPDATE and DELETE on them
    are blocked by SQLite triggers — not only by the UI.

  - Stock on hand is derived, never stored. It's the sum of stock
    movements. Impossible to drift.

  - Every write goes through an outbox. sync_queue holds pending
    changes for a future push. Sync never assumes the network is
    available.

  - The local SQLite is the source of truth. A server-side change
    arrives as a pull item and is applied locally; the desktop
    still works if the server goes down.

--------------------------------------------------------------
Development
--------------------------------------------------------------

  npm test          desktop tests (264 tests)
  npm start         desktop app

  cd server
  npm test          server tests (28 tests)
  npm start         server app

Test runner: node --test. No external test framework.

--------------------------------------------------------------
Project layout
--------------------------------------------------------------

  src/
    main/                 Electron main process
      db/                 SQLite init + migrations
      services/           All business logic
      ipc/                Thin IPC wrappers over services
    renderer/             UI (vanilla JS, no framework)
      views/              One file per screen
      state/              Shared state (session, language, sync dot)
      i18n/               Translations (English by default)
      styles.css          Design system
    shared/               Errors shared between main and renderer

  server/
    src/
      db.js               Postgres pool + migration
      store.js            PgStore: accept + pull
      app.js              Express routes
      index.js            Entry point
    test/                 Server tests

  test/                   Desktop tests

--------------------------------------------------------------
Extending
--------------------------------------------------------------

The pattern for every feature is:

  1. Write the test for the service behavior you want.
  2. Write the service in src/main/services/.
  3. Add the IPC channel in src/main/ipc/ and expose it in preload.js.
  4. Add the view on top of the tested IPC.

Every business rule lives in a service. Every service has tests.
The renderer is a thin layer: it calls window.kaupangr.* and renders
what comes back.

--------------------------------------------------------------
Language
--------------------------------------------------------------

The interface ships in English. Adding a second language:

  1. Copy src/renderer/i18n/en.js to src/renderer/i18n/xx.js.
  2. Translate the values on the right of each key.
  3. Add { code: 'xx', label: 'Your Language' } to
     src/renderer/i18n/languages.js.
  4. In src/renderer/i18n/index.js, import the new module and add
     it to "catalogues".

Restart the app and choose the language under Settings → Language.

